{"name":"Screech Owl","hex":"#eae4d8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"screech owl","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any New World owl of genus Megascops.","A common barn owl, of species Tyto alba, found almost worldwide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/screech_owl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Screech owl","blocks":[{"text":"Screech owls are typical owls (Strigidae) belonging to the genus Megascops with 22 living species. For most of the 20th century, screech owls were considered part of the genus Otus along with the Old World scops owls but were reassigned to Megascops in the 2000s based on a range of behavioral, biogeographical, morphological, and DNA sequence data."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/EasternScreechOwl-Rufous.jpg/330px-EasternScreechOwl-Rufous.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Screech_owl","wikidata":"Q945274"},{"title":"Owl","blocks":[{"text":"Owls are birds from the order Strigiformes, which includes over 200 species of mostly solitary and nocturnal birds of prey typified by an upright stance, a large, broad head, binocular vision, binaural hearing, sharp talons, and feathers adapted for silent flight. Exceptions include the diurnal northern hawk-owl and the gregarious burrowing owl."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG/330px-Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owl","wikidata":"Q25222"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Megascops_asio-gray.jpg/500px-Megascops_asio-gray.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Megascops_asio-gray.jpg/1280px-Megascops_asio-gray.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c